course: dictionary
lesson: 8860
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. The context, meaning the words and sentences around the word, tells you which meaning the writer wants. Shootout is one of these words.
explain: In a gun story, a shootout is a fight with guns between two people or groups. In sport, a shootout is a different thing: it is a way to decide the winner when a match ends in a draw.
explain: Read «England won Euro 2025, beating Spain on penalties (3–1 in the shootout) after a 1–1 draw.» Is the sentence about guns or about a game? A draw and penalties tell you it is sport, so swap in your meaning and check that it still makes sense.
fact: A shootout is a fight with guns between two people or groups.
fact: A penalty shootout decides a match that is drawn: players take turns to shoot at goal, and the team that scores more wins.
fact: In football and hockey, a shootout is used when a game is still level after the normal time.
fact: In a penalty shootout each side takes 5 kicks, then one each if it's still level.
fact: Useful phrases: «penalty shootout», «win on a shootout», «go to a shootout».
exercise: In a Wild West story, two cowboys have a shootout at noon. What does shootout mean here? | A race to the town well | A penalty shootout in football | A friendly game of cards | *A gunfight between two people
exercise: Tom says, «The match ended 1–1, so it went to a shootout.» What does shootout mean here? | A gunfight in a Wild West town | A long walk home after the game | *A way to decide the winner, with players taking turns to shoot at goal | A quarrel between two friends after school
exercise: What does a penalty shootout decide? | Who gets the biggest prize in a gunfight | Which player is the tallest | *Which team wins a match that was a draw | Who scores the first goal of the game
test: Which sentence uses shootout in its sport meaning? | *The match was 1–1, so they went to a shootout. | The shootout in the film had loud music. | The shootout between the two outlaws lasted one minute. | The cowboy drew his gun for the shootout at dusk.
test: «The keeper must keep part of one foot on or in line with the goal line until the ball is kicked.» In this sentence, what is the shootout? | A gunfight in a Wild West town | *A penalty shootout, where each side takes kicks | A race to the goal line | A fight between two football fans
exam: When a word has two meanings, what helps you pick the right one? | Choosing the longest meaning you can find | Ignoring the sentence and looking only at the title | *The context, meaning the words and sentences around it | Guessing from the first letter of the word
